Pair with `crossbeam_utils::sync::Parker` per consumer; producers fire the unparker:

```rust
use crossbeam_utils::sync::{Parker, Unparker};
use std::sync::Mutex;

pub struct BlockingMpmc<T> {
    queue: LockFreeMpmc<T>,
    parkers: Mutex<Vec<Unparker>>,
}

impl<T> BlockingMpmc<T> {
    pub fn push_blocking(&self, mut item: T) {
        loop {
            match self.queue.try_push(item) {
                Ok(()) => { if let Some(u) = self.parkers.lock().unwrap().pop() { u.unpark(); } return; }
                Err(returned) => { item = returned; std::thread::yield_now(); }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Each consumer creates its own `Parker`, registers the matching `Unparker` in the parkers vec, and parks on empty:

```rust
pub fn pop_blocking(b: &BlockingMpmc<i32>) -> i32 {
    let parker = Parker::new();
    let unparker = parker.unparker().clone();
    loop {
        if let Some(v) = b.queue.try_pop() { return v; }
        b.parkers.lock().unwrap().push(unparker.clone());
        if let Some(v) = b.queue.try_pop() { return v; } // recheck after registration
        parker.park();
    }
}
```

The recheck-after-register is the standard pattern to close the race between empty-check and registration. Without it, a producer can push and unpark before the consumer registers, causing a missed wakeup.

